{"book":{"id":"book-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba","slug":"the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba","title":"The Battle for Christmas: A Cultural History of America's Most Cherished Holiday","authors":[{"id":"person-stephen-nissenbaum","name":"Stephen Nissenbaum"}],"isbn13":["9780517472309"],"subjects":["American History"],"topics":["American Civil War","War & Military Strategy","World War II"],"centralFigures":[],"links":{"amazon":"https://www.amazon.com/s?k=The%20Battle%20for%20Christmas%3A%20A%20Cultural%20History%20of%20America's%20Most%20Cherished%20Holiday%20Stephen%20Nissenbaum","bookshop":"https://bookshop.org/search?keywords=The%20Battle%20for%20Christmas%3A%20A%20Cultural%20History%20of%20America's%20Most%20Cherished%20Holiday%20Stephen%20Nissenbaum","indiebound":"https://www.indiebound.org/search/book?keys=The%20Battle%20for%20Christmas%3A%20A%20Cultural%20History%20of%20America's%20Most%20Cherished%20Holiday%20Stephen%20Nissenbaum","worldcat":"https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=The%20Battle%20for%20Christmas%3A%20A%20Cultural%20History%20of%20America's%20Most%20Cherished%20Holiday%20Stephen%20Nissenbaum","publisher":"https://openlibrary.org/books/OL9835315M"},"sourceIds":["source-award-record-pulitzer-history-wikipedia-pulitzer-prize-for-history","source-cover-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba","source-open-library-isbn-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba","source-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba","source-publisher-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba"],"thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.132,"accessibilityScore":0.058,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"synthesis","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"from\"","catalog text contains \"history of\""]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"family\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.44,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Washington Irving","confidence":0.4},{"name":"Clement Clarke Moore","confidence":0.35}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Massachusetts","confidence":0.55},{"name":"New Yorkers","confidence":0.38},{"name":"America","confidence":0.35}],"argument":{"statement":"Modern complaints about Christmas commercialization echo earlier efforts to suppress excess; as urbanization intensified street revels, reformers shifted Christmas toward controlled family celebrations focused on children.","confidence":0.42}},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"9e05e8701314ec1046f6a10e09f65dd95815f142e990307dcc01999c9a48818b","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"American History","secondarySubjects":["Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book examines the historical and cultural evolution of Christmas celebrations in America, focusing primarily on societal changes and their impact on the holiday within a distinctly American context.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"1c8de9709b8ce1a6980cadb011635f4c0b97d18a2d5c2da2b7a76b916e109c5f","summary":"Americans who complain about the modern-day commercialization of Christmas may be surprised to discover that dissatisfaction with the way the holiday has been observed is by no means a new phenomenon. In 1659 the Massachusetts General Court declared the celebration of Christmas to be a criminal offense. What the Puritans were trying to suppress was a season of excess rooted in the ancient agricultural cycle - rowdy public displays of eating and drinking, mockery of established authority, aggressive begging, and boisterous invasions of the homes of the wealthy. In The Battle for Christmas, Stephen Nissenbaum shows how in the early nineteenth century, with the growth of cities, these Christmas-season carnival revels became even more threatening as they turned into gang violence and even riots. . Attempting to get Christmas out of the streets, a group of New Yorkers - Washington Irving among them - led a movement to transform it into a new style of celebration that would take place within the secure confines of the family circle, and be concerned especially with the happiness of children. We learn how two classic texts helped refashion the holiday: Clement Clarke Moore's \"A Visit from St. Nicholas\" and Charles Dickens's A Christmas Carol.","publicationYear":1988,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Christmas","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Weihnachten","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Kerstmis","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba"}],"displaySummary":"In 1659 the Massachusetts General Court declared the celebration of Christmas to be a criminal offense. What the Puritans were trying to suppress was a season of excess rooted in the ancient agricultural cycle - rowdy public displays of eating and drinking, mockery of established authority, aggressive begging, and boisterous invasions of the homes of the wealthy. In The Battle for Christmas, Stephen Nissenbaum shows how in the early nineteenth century, with the growth of cities, these Christmas-season carnival revels became even more threatening as they turned into gang violence and even riots. . Attempting to get Christmas out of the streets, a group of New Yorkers - Washington Irving among them - led a movement to transform it into a new style of celebration that would take place within the secure confines of the family circle, and be concerned especially with the happiness of children. We learn how two classic texts helped refashion the holiday: Clement Clarke Moore's \"A Visit from St. Nicholas\" and Charles Dickens's A Christmas Carol.","primaryTopic":"American Civil War","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"American History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"American History","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"American History","mappedSubject":"American History","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book examines the historical and cultural evolution of Christmas celebrations in America, focusing primarily on societal changes and their impact on the holiday within a distinctly American context.","id":"subject-evidence-book-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherished-holiday-stephen-nissenba-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubject":"History","score":3,"confidence":"medium","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-battle-for-christmas-a-cultural-history-of-america-s-most-cherishe